      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Social Capital Theory originated with Bourdieu's distinction between economic and social capital, which Coleman later refined through his analysis of trust and collective action in religious communities. Granovetter introduced the concept of the \"strength of weak ties,\" while Burt expanded this with his brokerage theory and structural holes, and Adler applied these ideas to organizational contexts. Putnam famously distinguished between bonding and bridging social capital in his work on civic engagement, a framework further developed by Sampson regarding neighborhood effects and collective efficacy. Lin's structural hole theory complements Burt's work, whereas Portes and Woolcock have critically examined the distributional consequences and institutional dimensions of social capital. Fukuyama's focus on trust as a prerequisite for cooperation aligns with Sen's broader capabilities approach, while Ostrom contributed significantly to the governance of common-pool resources.\n\nRecent scholarship by van der Lippe highlights gendered dimensions of social capital, and Cotton and Field have explored its role in economic development and rural communities. Waldinger's research on immigrant neighborhoods and Cross's work on social capital in diverse societies provide crucial empirical grounding. DiMaggio's analysis of cultural capital and institutional isomorphism intersects with the broader theory, while Kasza and Jorgensen have contributed to understanding",
